{"name":"Bog Fog","hex":"#bac3b9","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bog","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Bamako Sign Language."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An area of decayed vegetation (particularly sphagnum moss) which forms a wet spongy ground too soft for walking.","An acidic, chiefly rain-fed (ombrotrophic), peat-forming wetland."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bog"},{"term":"fog","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thick cloud that forms near the ground; the obscurity of such a cloud.","A mist or film clouding a surface."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become covered with or as if with fog.","To become obscured in condensation or water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fog"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bog","blocks":[{"text":"A bog or bogland is a wetland that accumulates peat as a deposit of dead plant materials – often mosses, typically sphagnum moss. It is one of the four main types of wetlands. Other names for bogs include mire, mosses, quagmire, and muskeg; alkaline mires are called fens. A bayhead is another type of bog found in the forest of the Gulf Coast states in the United States. They are often covered in heath or heather shrubs rooted in the sphagnum moss and peat. The gradual accumulation of decayed plant material in a bog functions as a carbon sink."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Lauhanvuori_suo.jpg/330px-Lauhanvuori_suo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bog","wikidata":"Q1681353"},{"title":"Fog","blocks":[{"text":"Fog is a visible aerosol consisting of tiny water droplets or ice crystals held in the air near the Earth's surface. Fog can be considered a type of low-lying cloud usually resembling stratus and is heavily influenced by nearby bodies of water, topography, and wind conditions. In turn, fog affects many human activities, such as shipping, travel, and warfare. Recent atmospheric research indicates that fog also functions as an active aquatic ecosystem. Fog droplets harbor microbiomes where bacteria actively grow, divide, and interact with atmospheric chemistry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg/330px-A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fog","wikidata":"Q37477"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/013_097_Isle_aux_Coudres.jpg/500px-013_097_Isle_aux_Coudres.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/013_097_Isle_aux_Coudres.jpg/1280px-013_097_Isle_aux_Coudres.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/013_097_Isle_aux_Coudres.jpg/500px-013_097_Isle_aux_Coudres.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Peat bog and peat to dry, L'Isle-aux-Coudres, Quebec, Canada, 1976","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A013_097_Isle_aux_Coudres.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/De_Liereman_prehistorische_kampplaats.jpg/500px-De_Liereman_prehistorische_kampplaats.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/De_Liereman_prehistorische_kampplaats.jpg/1280px-De_Liereman_prehistorische_kampplaats.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/De_Liereman_prehistorische_kampplaats.jpg/500px-De_Liereman_prehistorische_kampplaats.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bog in Antwerp Province, Belgi